DTSTART;TZID=Australia/Melbourne:20260827T111500
DTEND;TZID=Australia/Melbourne:20260827T123000
SUMMARY:Experimental/Behavioural – Ashley Craig (ANU) – Decoding Gender
  Bias in Interviews
LOCATION:Room 304\, FBE Building\, 111 Barry St\, Carlton VIC 3053
DESCRIPTION:Ashley Craig: “Decoding Gender Bias in Interviews”\nhttps:/
 /www.ashleycraig.com/\n\nZoom: https://unimelb.zoom.us/j/82654401351?pwd=V
 6TMN2ZpNRILVKO2Y5za5S7GjfbAGG.1&from=addon\n\nGoogle Maps: https://maps.ap
 p.goo.gl/BirLkJKurX9pSbBB8\n\nABSTRACT:\nPerformance evaluations in interv
 iews are central to employment decisions. We combine two field experiments
 \, administrative data and video analysis to study the sources of gender g
 aps in interview evaluations. Leveraging 60\,000 mock interviews on a plat
 form for software engineers\, we find that code quality ratings are 12 per
 cent of a standard deviation lower for women. This gap persists after cont
 rolling for an objective measure of code quality. Providing evaluators wit
 h automated performance measures does not reduce gender gaps. Comparing bl
 ind to non-blind evaluations without live interaction reveals no gender ga
 p in either case. In contrast\, gaps widen with longer personal interactio
 n and are larger among evaluators from regions with stronger implicit gend
 er bias. Video analysis shows that women apologize more\; and interviewers
  are more condescending and harsher with them. Both correlate with lower r
 atings. Our findings highlight how interpersonal dynamics can introduce bi
 as into evaluations that otherwise rely on objective metrics.
URL:https://unimelb.zoom.us/j/82654401351?pwd=V6TMN2ZpNRILVKO2Y5za5S7GjfbAG
 G.1&from=addon
END:VEVENT
