UID:4d7ead8355be60453e9975368146a487@generated
DTSTAMP:20260830T214404Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260830T214404Z
SEQUENCE:0
DTSTART;TZID=Australia/Melbourne:20261001T111500
DTEND;TZID=Australia/Melbourne:20261001T123000
SUMMARY:Experimental/Behavioural – Yiming Liu (WZB Berlin) – Model-Dire
 cted Attention and the Persistence of Wrong Mental Models: Evidence from F
 irm Pricing
LOCATION:Room 304\, FBE Building\, 111 Barry St\, Carlton VIC 3053
DESCRIPTION:Yiming Liu: “Model-Directed Attention and the Persistence of 
 Wrong Mental Models: Evidence from Firm Pricing”\nhttps://www.yimingliue
 con.com/\n\nZoom: https://unimelb.zoom.us/j/82654401351?pwd=V6TMN2ZpNRILVK
 O2Y5za5S7GjfbAGG.1&from=addon\n\nGoogle Maps: https://maps.app.goo.gl/BirL
 kJKurX9pSbBB8\n\nABSTRACT:\nWe show a way that wrong mental models can per
 sist in data-rich market environments: By remaining silent about data feat
 ures they cannot explain\, which directs attention away from disconfirming
  patterns. Studying 13\,000 gas station managers\, we show that 40 percent
  articulate a simple model — discounts raise sales — that is silent ab
 out intertemporal substitution (IS) by regular customers in response to pr
 e-announced temporary discounts\, evident in the data as sales dips before
  and after. Managers who neglect IS show measurable inattention to these d
 ips when explaining the data\, believe fuel demand is more elastic\, and s
 et lower prices. We also contribute evidence on how managers acquire the w
 rong model in the first place\, finding that it partly reflects newer mana
 gers absorbing it from peers and mentors. Online experiments confirm the c
 ausality involved in the model-directed attention explanation\, in both di
 rections: exposure to the simple peer model reduces attention to IS patter
 ns\, while a targeted attention intervention raises recognition of those p
 atterns and induces switching to the correct model.
URL:https://unimelb.zoom.us/j/82654401351?pwd=V6TMN2ZpNRILVKO2Y5za5S7GjfbAG
 G.1&from=addon
END:VEVENT
