SUMMARY:PhD Brown Bag Seminar – Adam Wang (University of Melbourne) – A
  Design Concept of Forecasting Software for Normalized Vector Autoregressi
 ons with Fat Tails and Stochastic Volatility
LOCATION:Room 304\, FBE Building\, 111 Barry St\, Carlton VIC 3056
DESCRIPTION:Adam Wang: “A Design Concept of Forecasting Software for Norm
 alized Vector Autoregressions with Fat Tails and Stochastic Volatility”\
 n\nZoom: https://unimelb.zoom.us/j/81347271549?pwd=DX1wTJkLbR8CVfWXiaW65kU
 hzrKAvS.1\n\nGoogle Maps: https://maps.app.goo.gl/BirLkJKurX9pSbBB8\n\nABS
 TRACT:\nWe present a suite of R packages for macroeconomic forecasting tha
 t leverages advanced Bayesian\, structural\, multivariate\, dynamic\, hier
 archical\, non-linear\, and non-Gaussian models. The suite enables both st
 ructural and predictive analyses\, and is adapted to time series data acro
 ss various types\, dimensions\, and sampling frequencies. Each additional 
 feature increases computational complexity. To address this challenge\, ou
 r software design incorporates a carefully curated selection of models\, e
 fficient algorithms implemented in C++\, advanced econometric and numerica
 l methods\, robust handling of complex input and output objects\, and stan
 dardised workflows. This approach combines the computational efficiency of
  C++ with the convenience of working with data in R. We demonstrate that o
 ur packages facilitate original research contributions in forecasting\, as
  illustrated by our example of enhanced density prediction and competitive
  point prediction for a monetary-fiscal system using vector autoregression
 s with non-centred stochastic volatility.
URL:https://unimelb.zoom.us/j/81347271549?pwd=DX1wTJkLbR8CVfWXiaW65kUhzrKAv
 S.1
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:76ee30091c5fe137f1a0005ab308c53a@generated
DTSTAMP:20260830T214404Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260830T214404Z
SEQUENCE:0
DTSTART;TZID=Australia/Melbourne:20260904T133000
DTEND;TZID=Australia/Melbourne:20260904T141000
SUMMARY:PhD Brown Bag Seminar – Jinjing Chen (University of Melbourne) 
 – Air Pollution and Road Safety: The Role of Agricultural Burning in Pak
 istan
LOCATION:Room 304\, FBE Building\, 111 Barry St\, Carlton VIC 3057
DESCRIPTION:Jinjing Chen: “Air Pollution and Road Safety: The Role of Agr
 icultural Burning in Pakistan”\n\nZoom: https://unimelb.zoom.us/j/813472
 71549?pwd=DX1wTJkLbR8CVfWXiaW65kUhzrKAvS.1\n\nGoogle Maps: https://maps.ap
 p.goo.gl/BirLkJKurX9pSbBB8\n\nABSTRACT:\nRoad traffic accidents impose sub
 stantial social costs\, especially in low- and middle-income countries\, y
 et the environmental determinants of accident risk remain poorly understoo
 d. This paper studies the effect of agricultural burning induced air pollu
 tion on road accidents in Punjab\, Pakistan\, from 2022 to 2025\, using ad
 ministrative emergency accident records. I combine VIIRS active-fire detec
 tions with HYSPLIT-simulated smoke plume trajectories to predict whether s
 moke from fires outside a local area is transported into it\, and use this
  predicted smoke exposure as an instrument for local air pollution exposur
 e. This approach allows me to estimate the causal effect of agricultural-b
 urning-induced air pollution on road accidents. I then examine the mechani
 sms underlying this effect by disentangling two channels: an air-pollution
  channel\, through which pollution may impair cognitive performance and dr
 iving behavior\, and a visibility channel\, through which smoke reduces si
 ght distance and worsens driving conditions. Finally\, I quantify the cont
 ribution of transboundary smoke from Indian Punjab and identify the upwind
  burn locations that generate the largest downstream road-safety externali
 ties\, with implications for spatially targeted enforcement.
URL:https://unimelb.zoom.us/j/81347271549?pwd=DX1wTJkLbR8CVfWXiaW65kUhzrKAv
 S.1
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bf5bb10d1f05b0ebe605436ec0dac9f2@generated
DTSTAMP:20260830T214404Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260830T214404Z
SEQUENCE:0
DTSTART;TZID=Australia/Melbourne:20260904T141000
DTEND;TZID=Australia/Melbourne:20260904T145000
SUMMARY:PhD Brown Bag Seminar – Kaiyue Chen (University of Melbourne) –
  Public EV Charging Stations in the Workplace
LOCATION:Room 304\, FBE Building\, 111 Barry St\, Carlton VIC 3058
DESCRIPTION:Kaiyue Chen: “Public EV Charging Stations in the Workplace”
 \n\nZoom: https://unimelb.zoom.us/j/81347271549?pwd=DX1wTJkLbR8CVfWXiaW65k
 UhzrKAvS.1\n\nGoogle Maps: https://maps.app.goo.gl/BirLkJKurX9pSbBB8\n\nAB
 STRACT:\nMidday electricity is increasingly cheap and clean\, making workp
 lace EV charging an attractive way to shift charging toward hours of abund
 ant renewable generation. But as electricity becomes cheaper\, scarcity sh
 ifts from energy to access to charging bays. Existing per-kWh prices do li
 ttle to allocate this scarce capacity: chargers are often allocated on a f
 irst-come\, first-served basis\, and drivers may continue occupying bays a
 fter charging is complete. We develop a model of parking pricing in which 
 drivers differ in their willingness to pay for charging and their costs of
  moving vehicles\, and study how parking prices affect whether drivers cha
 rge\, how long they occupy a charger\, and ultimately allocative efficienc
 y. We will later extend the model to endogenize arrival decisions and cong
 estion\, characterize the resulting equilibrium\, and use simulations to c
 ompare alternative policies in terms of social welfare.
URL:https://unimelb.zoom.us/j/81347271549?pwd=DX1wTJkLbR8CVfWXiaW65kUhzrKAv
 S.1
END:VEVENT
